
  That's the whole change to what exists: one function call and one script tag.

  [1mError handling[0m

  Expiry is checked lazily on read rather than by a timer — no background work, and the cost is only that a stale record
  sits in storage until something next reads it.

  [38;5;153mget()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m for all four failure modes: expired, corrupt JSON, unknown version, nothing stored. It clears the
  bad record as it goes, so a corrupt value fixes itself instead of failing forever.

  localStorage genuinely throws in private-browsing and storage-disabled contexts. Rather than let that break login, the
  module falls back to an in-memory record: the app keeps working, persistence quietly doesn't. Worth knowing that's
  the deliberate tradeoff — a user in private mode gets a session that dies on reload instead of an error.

  [1mTesting[0m

  This is the open question, because the project has no test runner, no linter, and no devDependencies. The logic worth
  testing is real — the expiry boundary, corrupt JSON, version mismatch, the storage-unavailable fallback — and it's the
  kind of thing that's quietly broken for months otherwise. Setting tooling up now is as cheap as it will ever be.

